course: dictionary
lesson: 8950
title: Silt: the rich mud rivers leave behind
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Silt is fine sand, mud and soil that flowing water carries along and then drops at the bottom of rivers, lakes or harbours. You can often work out what it means from the sentence around it. «The Blue Nile from Ethiopia brings most of the water and the rich silt that once flooded Egypt every year.» Rich silt is something good that water carries, and it flooded Egypt, so silt must be the muddy material the river leaves behind.
explain: Silt can also be a verb. «Some medieval ports have silted up, as Bruges did when trade moved to Antwerp.» To silt up means to become blocked with silt. A harbour that silts up is so full of mud that ships can no longer get in. Try the swap check: put «blocked with mud» in place of silted up, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Silt is a size as well as a material. Its grains are smaller than sand but bigger than clay. Sandy soil has big grains with big gaps, so water runs straight through it. Clay soil has tiny grains packed close together, so it holds water. Silt sits in between, and a good soil called loam mixes sand, silt, clay and humus.
fact: Silt is fine sand, mud and soil carried by flowing water.
fact: Rivers drop silt on flood plains, which makes the land fertile for farming, as along the Nile.
fact: «Silt up» means to become blocked with silt.
fact: Silt grains are smaller than sand but bigger than clay.
fact: «The Yellow River is named after the yellow-brown silt it carries», and that silt raises its bed so the river has changed course many times.
exercise: «The Blue Nile brought the rich silt that once flooded Egypt every year.» What is silt? | A kind of fish | *Fine sand, mud and soil carried by flowing water | Hard rock from mountains | Dry grass
exercise: Silt grains are smaller than sand but bigger than clay. Which type of grain is the smallest? | Sand | Silt | Pebbles | *Clay
exercise: «Some medieval ports have silted up, as Bruges did when trade moved to Antwerp.» What does silted up mean? | *Became blocked with silt | Filled with fresh fish | Became very deep and wide | Was sold to another town
test: «China's Yellow River builds up silt in its bed, raising the river.» What can happen when silt builds up in a river bed? | *The river rises and can flood | The water turns to ice | The river dries up completely | The river runs faster to the sea
test: Which soil holds water and can become sticky and waterlogged? | Gravel | Loam, the best for most crops | Sandy soil | *Clay soil
exam: Why is silt good for farmland? | Because it makes soil sandy so water drains fast | Because it turns soil into solid rock | Because it stops rivers from flowing | *Because it leaves rich soil on flood plains
